          "id": "2845901388",
          "author": "askhl",
          "body": "The best practice for reproducible random simulations is to create a seeded RNG (not touching the global one) and passing it around.  (See e.g. https://blog.scientific-python.org/numpy/numpy-rng/)\n\nWe aspire for all code to offer a default `rng` keyword which defaults to `np.random`, see e.g. `ase.md.velocitydistribution`.\n\nUnfortunately I can see that the minimahopping module does not offer an rng keyword, forcing the caller to seed the global generator.  Since that's unintended would you mind adding an `rng` keyword to minimahopping so the user can control randomness without touching the global generator?  Something like:\n\n```\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nMinimaHopping(..., rng=rng)\n```",
          "file_path": "examples/tutorials/constrained_minima_hopping.py",
          "line_number": 57,
          "commit_sha": "bf48a4e6cb4dd2b78febed6c17184f55159b41fe",
          "base_sha": "a620e93fd36fe78c6341df96acf8192cce85baf6",
          "created_at": "2025-10-26T22:36:49.694Z",
          "is_outdated": false
        },
